How to Apply These Terms to Your New Programs

If you develop a new program, and you want it to be of the greatest possible use to the public, the best way to achieve this is to make it free software which everyone can redistribute and change under these terms.

To do so, attach the following notices to each source file.  The safest way to do this is to start each source file with a dedicating comment header and an SPDX-License-Identifier line.

For files which contain only non-permissive terms (no AGPL notice, no AGPL additional restrictions), the comment header must also include the text "SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later" and a copyright line.  For files which contain part of the Program in object code form, the comment header should also include a statement that the source code is available under the AGPL-3.0-or-later, and a link to the source.

The exact form of the comment header depends on the file format.  Examples for C, Python, Java, and shell are below.

For C:

   // SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
   // Copyright (C) 2026 LiveAvatar Contributors

For Python:

   # SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
   # Copyright (C) 2026 LiveAvatar Contributors

For Java (note that the comment must be on the first lines):

   // SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
   // Copyright (C) 2026 LiveAvatar Contributors

For shell:

   # SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
   # Copyright (C) 2026 LiveAvatar Contributors

The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and modification follow.